---
import agentSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/agent.cpp?raw'
import chromiumSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/chromium.cpp?raw'
import embedSampleSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/embed-macos/main.mm?raw'
import rawSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/raw.c?raw'
import readWindowSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/read_window.cpp?raw'
import recordSource from '../../../../native/client/examples/record.cpp?raw'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { snippets } from '../../data/components.js'
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths.js'

const featured = [
  ['#form', 'Fill a form from C++', 'suitecut-native-agent'],
  ['#chrome', 'Drive the Chrome you already have', 'suitecut-native-chromium'],
  ['#c', 'Send one engine command from C', 'suitecut-native-command'],
  ['#record', 'Record a page with its sound', 'suitecut-native-record'],
  ['#reads', 'Keep several reads in flight', 'suitecut-native-read-window'],
  ['#cli', 'A session from the shell, or MCP', 'suitecut-native-control'],
  ['#embed', 'Show a tab in a Mac app', 'suitecut-embed-sample'],
] as const

const build = `cmake -S native/client -B .suitecut/examples-build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\
  -DSUITECUT_CLIENT_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON
cmake --build .suitecut/examples-build --parallel 2`

const runAgent = `suitecut-native-agent /absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser /tmp/form.png`
const runChromium = `suitecut-native-chromium /usr/bin/chromium https://example.com/`
const runRaw = `suitecut-native-command /absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser \\
  '{"command":"evaluate","expression":"40+2"}'`
const runReads = `suitecut-native-read-window /absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser 4 \\
  document.title location.href document.readyState`
const runEmbed = `suitecut-embed-sample --runtime /path/to/embed-kit --url https://example.com/`

// The sample is an 833-line AppKit app; its opening comment says what it does.
const embedHeader = embedSampleSource.slice(0, embedSampleSource.indexOf('#import'))
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Examples · suitecut"
  description="Complete C++, C and command-line examples from native/client/examples: drive a page, record it, call the C ABI, run an MCP server and embed a tab in a Mac app."
  current="examples"
>
  <article class="page prose">
    <h1>Examples</h1>
    <p>
      Each program here is printed whole from <code>native/client/examples</code> in the repository, and
      each builds into an executable of the same name. They take the browser's path as an argument and
      use only the public headers in <code>native/client/include/suitecut</code>.
    </p>
    <ul class="dp-rows">
      {
        featured.map(([href, title, detail]) => (
          <li>
            <a class="dp-link" href={href}>
              {title}
            </a>
            <span class="dp-detail">{detail}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={build} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />

    <h2 id="form">Fill a form from C++</h2>
    <p>
      Launch the engine, set a page's content, fill a field by its label and click a button by its
      role. The example checks that the click arrived as trusted input, then prints the page's
      accessibility observation and can save a screenshot.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={runAgent} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock code={agentSource} label="native/client/examples/agent.cpp" lang="cpp" />

    <h2 id="chrome">Drive the Chrome you already have</h2>
    <p>
      The same facade launches an installed Chrome or Chromium over a private pipe, without the
      engine. <code>Browser::launch_firefox</code> does the same for Firefox.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={runChromium} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock code={chromiumSource} label="native/client/examples/chromium.cpp" lang="cpp" />

    <h2 id="c">Send one engine command from C</h2>
    <p>
      A plain C program against <code>suitecut/client.h</code>. It launches the engine, sends any
      engine command object and prints the reply packet. Any language that can call C can do the
      same.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={runRaw} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock code={rawSource} label="native/client/examples/raw.c" lang="cpp" />

    <h2 id="record">Record a page with its sound</h2>
    <p>
      The recorder in the runtime archive. With the engine it sends the <code>recording</code> command
      and writes WebM, or hardware H.264 MP4 on macOS, with optional page audio. With
      <code>--backend chromium</code> it records a silent MP4 or WebM from an installed Chromium.
      <a href={sitePath('/docs/recording')}>Recording and streaming</a> explains the options.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={snippets.recordCode} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock code={recordSource} label="native/client/examples/record.cpp" lang="cpp" />

    <h2 id="reads">Keep several reads in flight</h2>
    <p>
      The transport under the facade accepts more than one request at a time. This example keeps a
      window of evaluations submitted, prints each result in order, and cancels what is still
      pending if one fails.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={runReads} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock code={readWindowSource} label="native/client/examples/read_window.cpp" lang="cpp" />

    <h2 id="cli">A session from the shell, or MCP</h2>
    <p>
      <code>suitecut-native-control</code> keeps a named browser session alive between commands. Each
      observation returns short refs that the next command can use.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      code={`${snippets.controlStart}\n${snippets.cliCode}`}
      label="Terminal"
      lang="bash"
    />
    <p>The same executable is a stdio MCP server:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={snippets.mcpConfig} label="MCP config" lang="json" />
    <p>
      <a href={sitePath('/docs/agents')}>Agents and MCP</a> covers tool groups, backends and the
      <code>SimpleAgent</code> class.
    </p>

    <h2 id="embed">Show a tab in a Mac app</h2>
    <p>
      The embed sample is a small AppKit app built with the client on macOS. It uses only
      <code>suitecut/embed.h</code> and draws the engine's IOSurface frames in a layer.
      <a href={sitePath('/docs/embedding')}>Embedding</a> walks through the API.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={runEmbed} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
    <CodeBlock
      code={embedHeader.trim()}
      label="native/client/examples/embed-macos/main.mm"
      lang="cpp"
    />
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main/native/client/examples/embed-macos/main.mm"
        >The full source</a
      > is on GitHub.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
